WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Are you good at math or stumped by basic algebra? Hopeless at spelling, but a great test taker? You may have a sense, perhaps instilled early on at school, of your academic ability as a fixed and immutable trait, something that you were born with like the color of your hair or eyes. But over the last several decades, research has suggested that thinking of intelligence this way can sap students' motivation in school and contribute to racial, gender, and socioeconomic achievement gaps. After all, a kid mind think, if I'm bad at math, why should I sign up for an advanced class? Or if I'm not really smart enough to be in college, what's the point of trying?
你擅长数学，还是被基础代数难倒了？对拼写一窍不通，却是个考试高手？你可能对自己的学术能力有一种看法，也许是早年在学校时就形成的，认为它是一种固定不变的特质，就像你的头发或眼睛的颜色一样与生俱来。但在过去几十年里，研究表明，用这种方式看待智力会削弱学生在学校的动机，并加剧种族、性别和社会经济地位方面的成就差距。毕竟，一个孩子可能会想，如果我数学不好，为什么还要报名高级课程呢？或者，如果我根本不够聪明上大学，那努力还有什么意义？

2
00:00:38.000 --> 00:01:19.000
In contrast, having a growth mindset, a belief that intelligence and ability can develop and increase with practice, can help keep students motivated when they face challenges. The concept of a growth mindset has made the leap from the psychology lab to popular culture and even become something of a buzzword in education. but it's also attracted controversy over how much of a difference growth mindset really makes to student achievement. So where is the research now? Can relatively simple interventions, which are exercises really, that reframe students thinking about their own abilities, help close achievement gaps? Whom do these interventions help most and how can they be deployed effectively?
相比之下，拥有成长型思维模式——即相信智力和能力可以通过练习不断发展和提高——可以在学生面对挑战时保持他们的动力。成长型思维模式的概念已经从心理学实验室跃升为流行文化，甚至成为教育界的一个流行词。但它也引发了争议，即成长型思维模式对学生成绩到底有多大影响。那么现在的研究进展如何呢？相对简单的干预措施——也就是重新构建学生对自己能力看法的练习——能否帮助缩小成就差距？这些干预措施对谁的帮助最大，又该如何有效地部署它们？

